WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.939
(Applause.)

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:14.570
HAASS: Well, good afternoon.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:19.470
I'm Richard Haass, and I want to welcome everyone
in the room and watching from afar to the

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.340
Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:27.861
And today we are fortunate and honored to
have with us the 25th secretary of defense-or,

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:33.290
if you prefer, SecDef of the United States,
Ashton Carter.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:39.250
Now, I realize ours is a country of about
320 million people, but if there ever was

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:43.290
one person meant to be secretary of defense,
it is Ash.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.290
(Laughter.)

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.820
He is the consummate scholar practitioner.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.810
He's held any number of influential positions
in the five-sided building, including that

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.870
of deputy secretary.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:59.110
But beyond that, he has degrees in medieval
history and theoretical physics.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:06.720
I find it difficult to think of a better preparation-(laughter)-oh,
you don't even know what I'm going to say.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.720
(Laughter.)

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.850
I find it hard to think of a better preparation
for understanding what is happening or could

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.710
come to happen in today's Middle East.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:22.990
He's about to take off-probably not on JetBlue-for
India and the Philippines, and that's a very

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.080
good thing.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.020
The Asia-Pacific region is the part of the
world where a lot of this century's history

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.130
will be written.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:36.290
Now, I understand the Middle East gets the
lion's share of the headlines, but time spent

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.220
in the Far East is time well spent.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.860
It's essential that the urgent now crowd out
the important.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:50.960
Full disclosure: Ash and I have been friends
and colleagues for over three decades now.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.159
We first got to know each other well in the
1980s when we were both faculty at Harvard's

00:01:55.159 --> 00:01:57.640
Kennedy School.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:02.760
One way of another, though, that collaboration
was destined to end in 1989 as either he was

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:09.561
heading off for a big job in the Dukakis administration
or I was going to work for then-Vice President

00:02:09.561 --> 00:02:10.619
George H.W.

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:11.619
Bush.

00:02:11.619 --> 00:02:15.109
The rest, as they say, is history.

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:16.109
(Laughter.)

00:02:16.109 --> 00:02:20.190
Today's meeting will begin with Secretary
Carter delivering extensive remarks on the

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.629
Asia-Pacific.

00:02:21.629 --> 00:02:26.920
This will then be followed by a conversation
between the secretary and Mary Boies, who

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:32.549
is, among other things, not just a prominent
lawyer but also a member of our board of directors

00:02:32.549 --> 00:02:38.279
and someone with extensive knowledge of and
experience with the U.S. military.

00:02:38.279 --> 00:02:44.060
Then the secretary will take a few questions,
after Mary, from you, our members.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:51.950
The plan is to wrap up by about 3:15 or, as
the secretary likes to call it, 15:15.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.359
With that, Secretary Carter.

00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:55.819
(Applause.)

00:02:55.819 --> 00:03:03.120
CARTER: Well, good afternoon, everyone.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.500
And, Richard, thanks.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.510
Thanks so much for those words, for decades
of friendship, for your leadership here at

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:18.540
the Council, and above all for your public
service, which continues.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.480
Richard is a member of the Department of Defense's
Defense Policy Board.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.469
We count on him for advice on all subjects
all the time.

00:03:26.469 --> 00:03:31.519
He and I were just talking shortly before
this about some things that he's preparing

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:35.170
to give us some advice on in just a few weeks'
time.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:40.019
It's nice to see a lot of old friends here
at the Council, many of you.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:45.109
I looked at the list of attendees coming and
it was like a homecoming in many, many ways-great

00:03:45.109 --> 00:03:50.449
to visit all my friends and be here at the
Council, because for generations the Council's

00:03:50.449 --> 00:03:56.329
hosted the debates and supported the thinkers
and ideas that have shaped America's relationship

00:03:56.329 --> 00:04:03.969
with the world, and those ideas are as important
as ever as we enter a new strategic era.

00:04:03.969 --> 00:04:10.659
Indeed, today's security environment is dramatically
different from the one that we have had for

00:04:10.659 --> 00:04:12.750
the last 25 years.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:21.500
In this era we face no fewer than five evolving
major immediate challenges: countering the

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:28.300
prospect of Russian aggression and coercion,
especially in Europe; managing historic change

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:35.330
in the vital Asia-Pacific region, including
China's rise, which we welcome, and some of

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:41.050
its actions, such as in the South China Sea,
about which we share the serious concerns

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:47.720
of all in the region; strengthening our deterrent
and defense forces in the fact of North Korea's

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:53.850
continued nuclear pursuits and provocations;
checking Iranian aggression and malign influence

00:04:53.850 --> 00:05:00.880
in the Gulf; and protecting our friends and
allies, especially Israel; and accelerating

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:08.370
the defeat of ISIL and its parent tumor in
Iraq and Syria and everywhere it's metastasizing

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:12.810
around the world, as well as protecting our
people here in the homeland.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:20.280
The United States and the Department of Defense
must and will address all five of those challenges.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.630
To do so is going to require some new strategic
and operational approaches, new force posture

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:33.410
in many places, and large investments in new
and enhanced capabilities-and all this we're

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:34.720
doing.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.280
But today I want to talk to you about how
we're meeting the challenges and seizing the

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:47.220
opportunities in the Asia-Pacific, and in
particular our growing security network there.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:54.450
Almost all the nations there are asking us
to do more with them, bilaterally and multilaterally.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:59.150
Tomorrow, as Richard said, I leave for India
and the Philippines, and I'm going to highlight

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:06.150
today some of the advances we'll be announcing
along the way.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:13.140
The Asia-Pacific is-and Richard made this
point-the single-most consequential region

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.580
of the world for America's future.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:22.311
We have long played an essential and pivotal
role in that region, and we're working today

00:06:22.311 --> 00:06:27.940
both individually and with our allies and
partners to ensure the Asia-Pacific remains

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:35.360
a region where everyone-everyone can rise
and prosper.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.820
That has been America's objective and practice
for decades.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:44.600
Regardless of what else was going on at home
or in other parts of the world, during Democratic

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:51.470
and Republican administrations, in times of
surplus and deficit, war and peace, the United

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:58.680
States has played a pivotal role economically,
politically, and militarily in the Asia-Pacific.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:04.910
Along with a wide variety of allies and partners,
for decades we have stood tall for enduring

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:10.370
principles, including peaceful resolution
of disputes, the freedom of navigation and

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:11.530
overflight.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:15.941
We've helped ensure that countries can make
their own security and economic choices, free

00:07:15.941 --> 00:07:19.970
from coercion and intimidation.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:24.980
And we have promoted free trade and the rule
of law to support development and unprecedented

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.050
growth.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:33.470
Of course, fundamentally sustaining this human
progress requires, as a foundation, security

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:39.460
and stability, and the United States has helped
provide both with its strong defense engagement

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:40.810
in the region.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:45.750
The highly capable men and women of the America
armed forces and our unique technology and

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:53.730
assets, coupled to our values, have long provided
the necessary reassurance-an attractive and

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:59.230
appealing reassurance-and worked to keep the
peace in the Asia-Pacific.

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:05.310
And because we have done so inclusively and
in a principled and respectful way, we have

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:09.350
developed alliances and partnerships all over
the region.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:14.570
These relationships, nurtured over decades,
tested in crisis and built on shared interests,

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:20.950
values, and sacrifice, form the bedrock of
our role in the Asia-Pacific, and accordingly

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.290
its stability and prosperity.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:30.810
The results have been extraordinary in the
interests of and to the benefit of all nations,

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.209
including the United States.

00:08:32.209 --> 00:08:38.110
Since World War II, millions have been lifted
from poverty and into the middle class.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:42.680
And even though there's still room for improvement,
democracy and freedom have spread to places

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:49.449
across the region, and economic miracle after
economic miracle has occurred in the region,

00:08:49.449 --> 00:08:51.439
first in Japan.

00:08:51.439 --> 00:08:58.730
Then Taiwan, South Korea, Southeast Asia rose
and prospered.

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:03.939
And now today China and India are doing the
same.

00:09:03.939 --> 00:09:08.459
This progress creates opportunities for the
region to continue to grow, but of course

00:09:08.459 --> 00:09:14.430
dramatic change can also produce some negatives,
and recently not all the news out of the Asia-Pacific

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:15.600
has been positive.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:23.050
Indeed, in the South China Sea, China's actions
in particular are raising regional tensions.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:28.709
That's why countries across the Asia-Pacific
are voicing concern with militarization, and

00:09:28.709 --> 00:09:35.970
especially over the last year with China's
actions, which stand out in size and scope.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:40.500
They're voicing those concerns publicly and
privately at the highest levels in regional

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.930
meetings and global fora.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:46.819
That's why many of these countries are reaching
out anew to the United States to uphold the

00:09:46.819 --> 00:09:51.339
rules and the principles that have allowed
the region to thrive.

00:09:51.339 --> 00:09:56.220
That's why we support intensified regional
diplomacy, not increased tensions, the threat

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:00.449
of force, or unilateral changes to the status
quo.

00:10:00.449 --> 00:10:05.579
And that's one reason why we're making enormous
investments in our capabilities, why so many

00:10:05.579 --> 00:10:11.589
are asking us to do more with them, and why
we'll continue to fly and sail and operate

00:10:11.589 --> 00:10:16.790
wherever international law allows, because
we must continue the progress that has helped

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:21.279
so many in the region to rise and prosper.

00:10:21.279 --> 00:10:24.850
President Obama launched the rebalance to
Asia-Pacific to ensure that we do our part

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:28.220
to make that bright opportunity come to pass.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:34.920
While I've, of course, absorbed myself with
the defense component of the rebalance, I

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:41.089
should be clear that one of the most important
strategic parts of the rebalance is the Trans-Pacific

00:10:41.089 --> 00:10:43.290
Partnership trade pact.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:49.680
TPP will bind the United States more closely
together with 11 other economies and unlock

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.899
economic opportunities for the United States
and many of its allies and partners.

00:10:53.899 --> 00:10:59.509
And TPP will help reinforce the open and inclusive
economic approach that has benefited so many

00:10:59.509 --> 00:11:01.610
in the Asia-Pacific.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:08.440
TPP should be ratified because of its economic
and strategic benefits and because we must

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:17.740
recognize what the alternative to TPP really
is-a regional economy, largest in the world,

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:25.290
with standards that don't serve American interests
and one that's carved up by lopsided, coercively

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:30.129
negotiated, lower-standard deals.

00:11:30.129 --> 00:11:34.759
That's why I've said that TPP is as strategically
important to the rebalance as an aircraft

00:11:34.759 --> 00:11:35.759
carrier.

00:11:35.759 --> 00:11:41.939
And I strongly urge Congress to approve TPP
this year.

00:11:41.939 --> 00:11:47.800
Militarily, the Department of Defense is operationalizing
the next phase of the rebalance and cementing

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.490
it for the long term.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:53.810
We're enhancing America's force posture throughout
this vitally important region to continue

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:58.990
playing a pivotal role from the sea and the
air and under the water, as well as to make

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:05.850
our posture more geographically distributed,
more operationally resilient, and more politically

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.370
sustainable.

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:13.279
To do so, we continue to bring the best people
and platforms forward to the Asia-Pacific,

00:12:13.279 --> 00:12:18.829
not only increasing the number of U.S. military
personnel in the region, part of some 365,000

00:12:18.829 --> 00:12:25.730
assigned to the Asia-Pacific today, but also
sending and stationing some of our most advanced

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:27.360
capabilities there.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:34.040
That includes F-22 and F-35 stealth fighter
jets, P-8 Poseidon maritime surveillance aircraft,

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:39.629
continuous deployments of B-2 and B-52 strategic
bombers, and also our newest surface warfare

00:12:39.629 --> 00:12:45.259
ships like the amphibious assault ship USS
America and all three of our newest class

00:12:45.259 --> 00:12:52.730
of stealth destroyers, the DDG-1000, which
will be all home-ported with the Pacific Fleet.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:57.720
And all the while we're bringing America's
regional force posture into the 21st century

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:03.920
by rotating American personnel into new and
more places like northern Australia and new

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.949
sites in the Philippines and modernizing our
existing footprint in Japan and the Republic

00:13:08.949 --> 00:13:10.290
of Korea.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:15.370
How we're doing this is a reflection of a
shift we're making across the Army, the Navy,

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:23.249
the Air Force, and the Marine Corps after
15 years of intensive effort in counterinsurgency

00:13:23.249 --> 00:13:29.360
in Iraq and Afghanistan toward full-spectrum
operations.

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.509
And in our 2017 defense budget, which I've
presented to Congress over the past few weeks,

00:13:33.509 --> 00:13:37.100
we're making investments critical to the rebalance.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:43.149
One is our surface fleet, which under our
budget grows both the number of ships and,

00:13:43.149 --> 00:13:49.420
importantly, above all, their capabilities
to deter and, if deterrence fails, defeat

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:55.300
even the most advanced potential naval adversaries
and protect the maritime security we all depend

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:56.300
on.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:00.129
Just one new example of how we're making our
ships' capabilities increasingly lethal is

00:14:00.129 --> 00:14:06.879
by maximizing production of the SM-6 missile,
one of our most modern and capable munitions,

00:14:06.879 --> 00:14:10.660
which now has a brand new anti-ship capability.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:11.660
And I could go on.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:16.220
We're also investing to ensure our continued
air superiority and global reach, including

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:21.800
with over $12 billion for the new B-21 long-range
strike bomber.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:27.570
Another investment is in undersea capabilities,
where we continue to dominate and where we're

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:34.319
investing over $8 billion just next year to
ensure ours is the most lethal and most advanced

00:14:34.319 --> 00:14:38.019
undersea and anti-submarine force in the world.

00:14:38.019 --> 00:14:45.350
That includes new undersea drones in multiple
sizes and diverse payloads that can importantly

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:50.389
operate in shallow waters, where manned submarines
can't.

00:14:50.389 --> 00:14:56.499
We're also making large new investments in
cyber, in electronic warfare, space capabilities,

00:14:56.499 --> 00:15:00.790
a total of $34 billion just next year.

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:05.459
Among other things, this will help us build
our cyber-mission force, develop next-generation

00:15:05.459 --> 00:15:11.629
electronic jammers, and prepare for the possibility
of a conflict that extends into space.

00:15:11.629 --> 00:15:16.050
And more is coming, including some surprises.

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:18.730
(Scattered laughter.)

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:22.610
We're also strengthening our alliances and
partnerships, which will be the focus of my

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:24.959
trip this coming week.

00:15:24.959 --> 00:15:27.870
And in that regard, let me take you on a brief
tour of what we're doing with partners around

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:32.079
the region and then focus on India and the
Philippines, where I'll be traveling next

00:15:32.079 --> 00:15:37.730
week, and important advances are being made
that occasion my trip.

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:41.910
Our alliances and partnerships are and will
remain one of our most important strategic

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.370
assets.

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:46.949
Our allies around the world, including those
in the Asia-Pacific, have stood with us and

00:15:46.949 --> 00:15:52.420
fought with us time and again, most recently
in Iraq, Afghanistan, and against ISIL.

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:55.209
And we're just as committed to them.

00:15:55.209 --> 00:15:59.600
As history has shown, we've fought with our
friends and allies and to defend the principles

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:04.299
and values we share in the Asia-Pacific, in
Europe, in the Middle East, and elsewhere.

00:16:04.299 --> 00:16:11.559
And while I can't take the time to detail
it today, both Japan and the Republic of Korea

00:16:11.559 --> 00:16:16.279
are strengthening their own militaries and
changing how they operate within our alliances

00:16:16.279 --> 00:16:19.420
in fundamental and forward-looking ways.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:24.129
We're sharing our best and newest capabilities,
including the F-35 Joint Strike Fighter, which

00:16:24.129 --> 00:16:29.119
will soon begin to operate with both Japan
and the Republic of Korea.

00:16:29.119 --> 00:16:37.639
Meanwhile, we're developing newer partnerships
with Vietnam, with Malaysia, with Indonesia,

00:16:37.639 --> 00:16:42.929
and others, while also enhancing our already
high-performing relationship with Singapore.

00:16:42.929 --> 00:16:49.129
And we're holding more numerous and more sophisticated
exercises with a growing network of partner

00:16:49.129 --> 00:16:54.119
countries, none larger than this summer's
RIMPAC, which will bring together more than

00:16:54.119 --> 00:17:00.970
two dozen navies, nearly twice the number
that participated just six years ago, to develop

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:09.079
the relationships that are critical to ensuring
the safety and security and peace of the region's

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:10.690
sea lanes.

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:16.560
You can see the breadth and depth of our bilateral
efforts with one of our key growing partners,

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:21.060
India, where I'll arrive a short time from
right now.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:27.370
The U.S.-India relationship is destined to
be one of the most significant partnerships

00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:29.710
of the 21st century.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:36.200
Ours are two great nations that share a great
deal-democratic governments, multiethnic and

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:42.640
multicultural societies with a commitment
to individual freedom and inclusivity, and

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:46.800
growing, innovative, open economies.

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:52.060
Over the course of my years at the Defense
Department, I've seen a remarkable convergence

00:17:52.060 --> 00:17:59.090
of U.S. and Indian interests, what I call
a strategic handshake.

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:05.660
As the United States is reaching west in its
rebalance, India is reaching east in Prime

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:12.120
Minister Modi's Act East policy that will
bring it farther into the Indian and Pacific

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:13.120
Oceans.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:16.691
We see this handshake reflected in the Joint
Strategic Vision Statement that President

00:18:16.691 --> 00:18:22.800
Obama and Prime Minister Modi released last
January and the 2015 framework for the U.S.-India

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:27.250
defense relationship, also a new thing, which
Indian Defense Minister Parrikar and I signed

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:29.750
last year in Delhi.

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:34.310
The defense framework is foundational, and
it's going to guide the U.S.-India defense

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:37.370
relationship for the next decade.

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:41.260
And there's another handshake between our
countries as well, a technological one.

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:46.600
In 2012 the United States and India created
the Defense Technology and Trade Initiative

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:52.490
to leverage the convergence between our industrial
and technological abilities in an unprecedented

00:18:52.490 --> 00:19:00.440
way, this after a half a century since Indian
independence of separation of our militaries

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:03.620
and our industrial systems.

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:10.880
That initiative grasps hands with Prime Minister
Modi's Make in India campaign to expand the

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:12.880
nation's industrial and defense base.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:19.690
And it will lead to greater production-co-production,
excuse me-and co-development of defense capabilities.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:24.310
While in India, I'll meet with Prime Minister
Modi and also Defense Minister Parrikar to

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:30.730
discuss the progress we've made together in
aircraft-carrier, jet-fighter, and jet-engine

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:31.730
collaboration.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:35.280
And we'll talk about exciting new projects,
the details of which I can't go into this

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:40.290
afternoon, but stay tuned when I'm with Minister
Parrikar.

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:45.060
There's so much potential here, which is why
we're seizing every opportunity we can.

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:48.870
Last year the Modi government reached out
to the United States to discuss the possibility

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:53.790
of launching joint production on a new platform
to build on the work Lockheed Martin and Indian

00:19:53.790 --> 00:20:00.310
industry achieved on the C-130J project, and
what Boeing and Indian industry will achieve

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:06.800
in the production of Apache and Chinook helicopters
in India, recently purchased.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:12.810
Members of my team and industry are right
now, as we are here in New York, in India

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:17.260
looking at the potential co-production of
fighter aircraft.

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:21.090
These conversations represent the growing
enthusiasm of U.S.-India partnership.

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:23.470
And even more than that, its promise.

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:28.310
While these negotiations can be difficult,
and global competition is high, I have no

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:32.520
doubt that in the coming years the United
States and India will embark on a landmark

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:37.830
co-production agreement that will bring our
two countries closer together and make our

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:40.440
militaries stronger.

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:44.880
As our strategic and technological interests
are drawn together, so too have our military

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.200
ties.

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:51.071
We're coming together operationally across
domains by air, land, and sea, to collaborate

00:20:51.071 --> 00:20:57.780
on humanitarian assistance, disaster relief,
maritime security, and maritime domain awareness.

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:02.620
This week will also conclude several important
agreements, including one on commercial shipping

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:09.920
information exchange, which will make many
new things possible in the future.

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:15.750
Our gathering partnership in defense can also
be seen in India's return to major joint exercises,

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:21.280
like Red Flag, our prestigious U.S. Air Force-hosted
aerial combat training exercise where all

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:26.870
our top pilots, and those from countries like
India, train together.

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:31.400
India will again participate in RIMPAC, which
I mentioned, the world's largest international

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:32.890
maritime exercise.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:39.910
And in the Malabar exercise, Japan, India,
and the United States, all three, have operated

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:47.170
together at sea in such critical training
as air defense and anti-submarine warfare.

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:54.830
From India, I'll travel to the Philippines,
with which we have one of our longest relationships

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:56.010
in the region.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:22:01.160
We share much history and many common ties
with the Philippines, and our long-running

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:05.400
defense alliance has been a cornerstone of
peace and stability in the region for more

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:07.230
than 65 years.

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:13.320
And as President Obama has made clear, our
commitment to the Philippines is ironclad.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:17.940
Today our alliance is as close as it's been
in many years, thanks to two major recent

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:23.790
steps forward that occasion my visit in coming
days.

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:28.870
First is the U.S.-Philippines Enhanced Defense
Cooperation Agreement, the so-called EDCA,

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:37.220
which is a landmark agreement that was ratified
in the Philippines in January.

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:44.010
And also, the new U.S. Southeast Asia Maritime
Security Initiative, by which we-that is,

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:49.141
the Department of Defense-is helping to fund
these changes in the Philippines and other

00:22:49.141 --> 00:22:50.530
partners in the region.

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:56.010
As part of EDCA's implementation, we're supporting
modernization of the Philippine armed forces,

00:22:56.010 --> 00:23:01.440
and strengthening mutual defense, an arrangement
that will allow our forces, at the invitation

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:07.480
of the government of the Philippines, to conduct
regular rotational training exercises and

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:08.580
activities.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:16.610
We recently announced an initial slate of
five agreed locations for those alliance activities.

00:23:16.610 --> 00:23:18.820
Locations are arrayed throughout the archipelago.

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:24.140
They will offer the opportunity for increasingly
complex bilateral engagements.

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:28.520
And I plan to visit two of those locations
next week.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:34.480
First, Fort Magsinghsingh (sic; Magsaysay)
and Antonio Bautista Air Force--saysay, I

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:38.820
should say-and Antonio Bautista Air Force
Base.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:47.350
At Fort Magsaysay, the former home of the
Philippines army's premier training facility,

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:51.790
we previously made use of limited preposition
disaster relief supplies that supported our

00:23:51.790 --> 00:23:56.010
response to Typhoon Haiyan in 2013.

00:23:56.010 --> 00:24:02.430
EDCA will enable us to augment these stocks
and add new ones to the air base, thereby

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:05.130
improving our ability to respond to future
disasters.

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:11.360
I'll be discussing both of these things at
these sites when I arrive in the Philippines.

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:16.760
And last year, at the region's Shangri-La
Dialogue, I announced our Maritime Security

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:17.790
Initiative.

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:24.660
This initiative represents a $425 million,
five-year commitment by the Defense Department

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:29.620
to help countries like the Philippines share
information, identify potential threats, and

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:34.940
work collaboratively to address common challenges
in the region.

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:39.370
We've just released the first tranche of this
money, nearly 80 percent of which is going

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:41.110
to the Philippines.

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:47.540
There, it will help modernize the technology
and train the staff at the Philippines National

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:52.210
Coast Watch Center, enhance an information
network to enable the sharing of classified

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:58.070
communications between the U.S. Pacific Command
and Hawaii and key Philippine maritime command

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:03.260
centers, provide an aerostat reconnaissance
platform, and outfit the Philippine navy patrol

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:08.490
vessels with better sensors so they can do
more in the region's waters.

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:13.490
These two things, EDCA and the Maritime Security
Initiative, will take the U.S.-Philippines

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:19.710
alliance capability to a new level, one that
has not been seen in decades.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:26.850
I will also see some of this first-hand at
Exercise Balikatan 2016, our premier exercise

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:28.040
with the Philippines.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:34.650
Balikatan is going on right now, and includes
over 7,000 personnel from every military service

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:39.800
in both countries, dozens of American aircraft,
vehicles and vessels, including one of our

00:25:39.800 --> 00:25:45.720
aircraft carriers and several important components,
including a simulated gas and oil platform

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:48.980
recovery raid in the South China Sea.

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:52.520
Balikatan signals shared resolve.

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:55.370
It enhances our shared capabilities.

00:25:55.370 --> 00:26:01.040
And it demonstrates, once again, America's
dedication to standing shoulder-to-shoulder

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:03.870
with the Philippines.

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:08.230
Like our alliance with the Philippines and
our developing partnership with India, America's

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:13.710
bilateral relationships remain the bedrock
of our presence and engagement in the Asia-Pacific.

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:19.220
But in a large and interconnected region,
especially one with so many strong, capable,

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:25.530
and dedicated players, it makes sense to network
and link relationships and produce gains for

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:26.530
all.

00:26:26.530 --> 00:26:27.910
And that's what we're doing.

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:33.080
Now, unlike-I mean-excuse me.

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:38.330
Unlike elsewhere in the world, peace and stability
in the Asia-Pacific has never been maintained

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:42.110
by a region-wide alliance, like NATO.

00:26:42.110 --> 00:26:45.190
No comparable formal structure.

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:50.790
That's made sense for the Asia-Pacific, with
its unique history, geography, and politics.

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:55.350
Instead, regional security, stability, and
prosperity have required nations working together

00:26:55.350 --> 00:26:57.000
less formally.

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:04.290
And the United States has been an important
builder, cement, and participant in this arrangement.

00:27:04.290 --> 00:27:09.320
Today, as the region changes, the United States
is augmenting our bilateral relationships

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:14.110
and alliance with trilateral and multilateral
arrangements.

00:27:14.110 --> 00:27:20.490
We're weaving these partnerships together
to more effectively bolster American and regional

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:21.490
security.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:28.160
This network, with its shared values, habits
of cooperation, and compatible and complementary

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:34.580
capabilities, will expand the reach of all,
responsibly share the security burden, and

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:38.420
help ensure peace and stability in the region
for years to come.

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:40.900
This burgeoning networks builds in three ways.

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:46.200
First, the Department of Defense is strongly
emphasizing trilateral mechanisms to bring

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:52.700
together like-minded allies and partners to
maximize individual contributions to regional

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:59.570
peace and security, and link together nations
that previously worked with us mostly separately.

00:27:59.570 --> 00:28:07.530
For example, the U.S.-Japan-Korea trilateral
partnership helps us all work together, as

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:13.779
well as share information and coordinate responses
to, for example, North Korean provocations.

00:28:13.779 --> 00:28:17.960
Last month's trilateral meeting between President
Obama, Prime Minister Abe, and President Park

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.360
on this topic was historic.

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:27.980
The U.S.-Japan-Australia relationship is also
expanding practical cooperation, enhancing

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:31.750
exercises, training, and information sharing,
and building capabilities.

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:38.840
And our burgeoning U.S.-Japan-India trilateral
relationship is evolving from the strategic

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:46.130
dialogue, where it began, through joint activities,
like the Malabar Exercise, to real, practical

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:53.180
security cooperation that spans the entire
region, from the subcontinent right around

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:55.210
to East Asia.

00:28:55.210 --> 00:29:00.400
Second, to improve regional security, we're
encouraging our allies and partners to actively

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:04.320
develop their own interconnected security
relationships.

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:07.560
Many countries within the Asia-Pacific are
strengthening their bilateral relationships

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:13.800
with one another in enhanced ways that we
think also enhance regional stability.

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:16.680
But they're also creating their own trilateral
arrangements.

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:23.790
The Japan-Australia-India trilateral meeting
last June, for example, was a welcome and

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.350
brand-new development.

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:32.350
Third, we're helping create an interconnected
regional architecture, from one end of the

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:37.440
region to another, through engagement and
activities in multilateral fora, such as the

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.950
ASEAN Defense Ministers Meeting.

00:29:39.950 --> 00:29:43.880
In this way, the United States is helping
strengthen relationship and building partner

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:49.390
capabilities on key issues, like maritime
security, humanitarian assistance, and disaster

00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:50.390
relief.

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:55.260
Singapore has shown great leadership in this
area by hosting an operations center to coordinate

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:56.910
activities across ASEAN.

00:29:56.910 --> 00:30:02.530
It's important to remember that our bilateral,
trilateral, and multilateral relationships,

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:08.800
and the developing network they comprise,
is not aimed at any particular country.

00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:16.460
Rather, it demonstrates that the region wants
cooperation, not coercion; and a continuation,

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:22.590
not an end, of decades of stability, peace,
and progress.

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:24.200
The network's not closed.

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:26.350
It excludes no one.

00:30:26.350 --> 00:30:31.160
We want cooperation and shared leadership
so that other nations who want to contribute

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:36.180
to regional stability and security, they can
work with the other nations of the region

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:37.310
to do so.

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:42.660
For example-important example-we have disagreements
with China, but we are committed to working

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:46.120
through them in ways that do not destabilize
the region.

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:50.301
That's one reason why we will continue to
pursue a military-to-military relationship

00:30:50.301 --> 00:30:58.590
with China, focused on risk reduction and
practical cooperation in areas of mutual interest.

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:03.890
This network overall demonstrates the United
States' commitment to remaining a pivotal

00:31:03.890 --> 00:31:08.500
and essential leader in the Asia-Pacific for
decades to come.

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:13.580
By operationalizing the rebalance, by transforming
old alliances and new partnerships, and by

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:20.000
networking security, we can gather force and
respond to any manner of crisis, manmade or

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:24.690
natural disaster, and continue to promote
and defend the principles that have allowed

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:30.790
so many in the region to rise and prosper
for so long.

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:35.630
Let me close with a few words about who makes
that possible.

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:41.880
As I stand here, there are hundreds of thousands
of men and women serving and defending our

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:47.660
country-right now, in every time zone, every
domain, in the air, ashore, and afloat.

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:50.720
They are the ones who are operationalizing
the rebalance.

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:53.140
They're making the network work.

00:31:53.140 --> 00:31:55.020
They need always to be in our minds.

00:31:55.020 --> 00:32:01.410
You know, they say that security-frequently
this is said in this region-is like oxygen:

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:07.710
when you have enough of it, you pay no attention
to it; but when you don't have enough, you

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:10.620
can think of nothing else.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:17.520
America's service members provide that oxygen-the
security that allows millions upon millions

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:21.730
of people, not just in America and not just
in the Asia-Pacific, but in so much of the

00:32:21.730 --> 00:32:27.180
world to be safe, to raise their children,
to dream their dreams, to live lives that

00:32:27.180 --> 00:32:28.180
are full.

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:32.431
Our soldiers, sailors, airmen, and Marines
take grave risks to provide that security,

00:32:32.431 --> 00:32:33.900
and some make the ultimate sacrifice.

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:38.870
They do so not just because they were ordered
to, and they do so not only because they want

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:39.980
to protect their buddies.

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:45.270
They do so because they know that they help
make the world a better place through American

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:47.000
leadership.

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:51.430
In a new strategic era and at a time of great
change, we must and will continue to play

00:32:51.430 --> 00:32:55.440
that essential, principled role in the Asia-Pacific.

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:58.330
We will work with new partners and old allies.

00:32:58.330 --> 00:33:00.290
We'll network our security relationships.

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:02.830
We will invest and innovate.

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:06.740
We'll change how we plan, how we operate,
even how we fight.

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:10.940
But we will never change what we're willing
to fight for: for our safety and freedoms,

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:15.900
and that of our friends and allies; and for
the values, principles, and rule-based order

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:21.670
that produced security, stability, and prosperity
for all.

00:33:21.670 --> 00:33:27.220
Because we do so, we will continue to ensure
that the Asia-Pacific remains a region where

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:34.720
everyone can rise and prosper for generations
to come.

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:40.140
Thank you.

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:42.860
(Applause.)

00:33:42.860 --> 00:33:51.790
BOIES: Thank you, Mr. Secretary.

00:33:51.790 --> 00:33:52.809
CARTER: Thank you, Mary.

00:33:52.809 --> 00:33:58.520
BOIES: And welcome to everyone at the CFR
Corporate Conference.

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:05.270
You stated in the defense posture that was
released, I believe, last month that-and I'm

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:12.860
going to quote-"We expect China to uphold
President Xi's pledge not to pursue militarization

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:16.500
of the Spratly Islands of the South China
Sea."

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:22.980
There are reports of mounting evidence that
the Chinese are doing in the Scarborough Shoals

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:28.169
exactly what they were doing in the Spratly
Islands; that is, namely, building an island

00:34:28.169 --> 00:34:33.220
around some kind of maritime feature or rock.

00:34:33.220 --> 00:34:39.899
The shoals have-are also claimed by the Philippines,
who have brought an arbitration in The Hague.

00:34:39.899 --> 00:34:44.610
The shoals are 140 miles off the coast of
Manila.

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:47.540
Has the president of China broken his pledge?

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:50.500
CARTER: Well, we'll see.

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:52.770
But the president of China did make that pledge.

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:59.720
But let me back up a little bit because the
South China Sea is a place that's very much

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:05.400
in the focus of many in the region, to which
I'll be traveling over the next few days.

00:35:05.400 --> 00:35:14.930
China is one of many claimants to various
features throughout the region, many of which

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:19.300
have taken steps that we oppose, namely militarization.

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:25.300
But over the last year, the scale and scope,
as I indicated, of China's have been-have

00:35:25.300 --> 00:35:27.120
outstripped anybody else's.

00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:32.190
The United States' position on this is we
don't take any position on the claims themselves,

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:37.100
but we do take a position on militarization
by anyone, but especially by China in this

00:35:37.100 --> 00:35:39.320
particular context.

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:48.680
It won't affect our operations, but it disquiets
the region when this occurs.

00:35:48.680 --> 00:35:55.150
It has the effect of, of course, causing us
to react-which will be to-in the ways that

00:35:55.150 --> 00:36:02.570
I described in terms of our own preparations-but
also of having what I also described, which

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:06.260
is many countries in the region want to work
with us more.

00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:08.050
So that's the effect of this.

00:36:08.050 --> 00:36:16.290
But, you know, our approach, Mary, is that
things in the South China Sea should be a

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:20.770
reflection of our overall approach to the
region, which is this is a region that has

00:36:20.770 --> 00:36:27.040
had it good for 70 years, and this would be
a serious mistake for all of us to allow that

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:31.940
to unravel, and for militarization to lead
to tension to lead to conflict.

00:36:31.940 --> 00:36:34.860
So we're trying to prevent that.

00:36:34.860 --> 00:36:43.080
That's the position we come to this from,
and we certainly have been given to believe

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:48.510
that the president of China shares that view
and made that commitment.

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:50.420
But we'll see.

00:36:50.420 --> 00:36:54.680
BOIES: Critics have argued that our response
has been rather weak.

00:36:54.680 --> 00:37:03.510
We have done some freedom of navigation operations
within the 12-mile limit of features or islands

00:37:03.510 --> 00:37:10.570
that China declares are theirs, but then we've
confused the issue by characterizing them

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:16.990
as innocent passage, which does not suggest
that we're disputing the ownership.

00:37:16.990 --> 00:37:23.630
And we've had various of these operations,
and they don't seem to have a clear signal.

00:37:23.630 --> 00:37:25.200
Is there a reason for that?

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:29.710
CARTER: Well, their purpose isn't to signal;
their purpose is they've been going on for

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:32.290
decades now, and we continue to do that.

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:36.630
There are differences between different kinds
of passage, as you indicate.

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:39.350
We know what we're doing.

00:37:39.350 --> 00:37:46.520
But, I mean, the basic point here is that
the United States, here and everywhere around

00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:53.820
the world-whether it's the Arctic, whether
it's the Horn of Africa-we, along with virtually

00:37:53.820 --> 00:37:59.810
everyone else, insists upon freedom of navigation
consistent with international law.

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:01.060
So that's how we operate.

00:38:01.060 --> 00:38:03.350
And you say, what are we doing more generally
in the region?

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:08.120
Well, and that's what I was describing there,
which is trying to work with others, to include

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:18.530
China-and that's an important point-to widen
the network of security cooperation in a region

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:25.790
where, remember, there is no NATO, where the
wounds of the past never healed.

00:38:25.790 --> 00:38:27.360
And so it's important.

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:31.460
And the United States plays an important role,
both with respect to China, but also with

00:38:31.460 --> 00:38:33.400
respect to Japan, South Korea, India.

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:36.720
I mean, just walk around.

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:38.680
And that's what we're doing there.

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:39.790
We're committed to that.

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:42.130
We've played that role for decades.

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:45.530
We want to keep a good thing going-we and
everybody else in the region.

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:50.041
BOIES: And there are many areas in which we
have found a lot of cooperation with China-

00:38:50.041 --> 00:38:51.041
CARTER: Sure.

00:38:51.041 --> 00:38:56.800
BOIES: -in areas of climate change; perhaps
in the areas of cyber-it's probably too early

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:00.860
to see there; certainly to an extent with
North Korea.

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:07.530
Although North Korea nonetheless still seems
to be persisting in developing its programs.

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:13.620
It did a nuclear warhead test in January,
more missile testing in February just of this

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:15.220
year.

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:19.340
And question there: Is our deterrence working?

00:39:19.340 --> 00:39:20.680
It doesn't seem to be.

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:28.170
CARTER: Well, our deterrence of conflict has
worked for 60 years, and we continue to strengthen

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:29.170
that.

00:39:29.170 --> 00:39:35.300
We have a slogan for U.S. Forces Korea, which
stand on the DMZ every day: "Fight tonight."

00:39:35.300 --> 00:39:39.810
That's not that they want to fight tonight,
but they're ready to fight tonight.

00:39:39.810 --> 00:39:46.700
That's why we're doing so much-and I won't
go into it here, but you can read about it-to

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:52.560
strengthen and transform the U.S.-ROK alliance
really in fundamental ways: its command and

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:57.170
control, its basic structure, its approach
to deterrence on the Korean Peninsula.

00:39:57.170 --> 00:40:01.710
And that deterrence has been strong now for
50, 60 years.

00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:03.540
We intend to keep it that way.

00:40:03.540 --> 00:40:11.490
Now, it presents new challenges for deterrence
when the North Koreans build missiles and

00:40:11.490 --> 00:40:12.990
test nuclear weapons.

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:20.260
And to get to your point about China, you
know, China has been a party to the talks

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:25.470
in which people in the region-not just us
but China also, South Korea, Japan, and also

00:40:25.470 --> 00:40:29.470
Russia-have tried to get the North Koreans
to turn back.

00:40:29.470 --> 00:40:36.090
China has, logically speaking, by far and
away the greatest leverage over North Korea

00:40:36.090 --> 00:40:41.560
by dint of geographical proximity, economic
relations, and so forth.

00:40:41.560 --> 00:40:47.490
They have been unsuccessful so far, and certainly
we have been unsuccessful in getting North

00:40:47.490 --> 00:40:48.720
Korea to stop.

00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:56.070
What that's causing us to do is we are reacting
in terms of how we're protecting ourselves,

00:40:56.070 --> 00:41:01.370
our forces on the peninsula-that's what the
THAAD missile defense system is about on the

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:15.990
Korean Peninsula-and various changes we're
making to our defensive strategy and our deterrent

00:41:15.990 --> 00:41:17.650
plans on the Korean Peninsula.

00:41:17.650 --> 00:41:22.130
We don't like to have to be doing that, but
we're doing that and we have to do that, as

00:41:22.130 --> 00:41:25.070
we have for decades, because we've got to
keep deterrence strong.

00:41:25.070 --> 00:41:33.530
BOIES: You mentioned the THAAD system, the
Terminal High Altitude Defense system, and

00:41:33.530 --> 00:41:39.520
that is an area where the Chinese now are
vigorously objecting should that system be

00:41:39.520 --> 00:41:45.010
deployed in South Korea, which, at the present
time, it is not.

00:41:45.010 --> 00:41:53.100
Tell us what the risks and benefits that you
see in that particular endeavor.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:57.000
Is it going to happen or are we going to back
down?

00:41:57.000 --> 00:41:58.000
CARTER: Oh, it's going to happen.

00:41:58.000 --> 00:41:59.000
No, it's a necessary thing.

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:00.350
It's between us and South Korea.

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:05.750
It's part of protecting our own forces on
the Korean Peninsula and protecting South

00:42:05.750 --> 00:42:06.750
Korea.

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:08.119
It has nothing to do with the Chinese.

00:42:08.119 --> 00:42:15.510
And I do wish the Chinese would work with
us, or really work bilaterally with North

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:20.340
Korea more effectively-although it's easy
to say that but dealing with North Korea for

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:25.970
anybody is a challenge-at heading off their
missile challenge in the first place.

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:27.520
But we need to defend our own people.

00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:29.460
We need to defend our own allies.

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:31.090
And we're going to do that.

00:42:31.090 --> 00:42:37.130
BOIES: The Chinese ambassador to South Korea
said that should the THAAD missile system

00:42:37.130 --> 00:42:44.270
be deployed in South Korea, that will-it was
translated "in an instant" end the good bilateral

00:42:44.270 --> 00:42:49.580
relations that have been built up over at
least the last three years between China and

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:50.580
South Korea.

00:42:50.580 --> 00:42:51.940
Is that just an idle threat?

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:54.310
CARTER: Well, that would be sort of illogical.

00:42:54.310 --> 00:43:03.210
I can't speak for a Chinese ambassador, but
we're actually quite encouraged to see the

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:09.090
Republic of Korea and China strengthening
their own relationship.

00:43:09.090 --> 00:43:13.950
That's had its own ups and downs, and when
you go back in the history of this whole region

00:43:13.950 --> 00:43:19.300
there's a lot of history there, including
between China and Korea.

00:43:19.300 --> 00:43:26.010
So actually we think that's a good thing and
part of this network of security that we encourage-China,

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:29.510
South Korea, and us all together.

00:43:29.510 --> 00:43:39.050
Obviously North Korea isn't joining that right
now and I can well see why it's concerning

00:43:39.050 --> 00:43:41.450
to the Chinese.

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:48.070
But I think you've seen in President Park
and President Xi, as well as President Obama,

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:55.450
as I mentioned, a real desire to join Korea-now,
South Korea I'm talking about and China, and

00:43:55.450 --> 00:43:57.270
get over some of their previous history.

00:43:57.270 --> 00:43:58.640
We're supporters of that.

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:03.050
BOIES: Do we have a red line with North Korea?

00:44:03.050 --> 00:44:09.990
They do not yet have the capability of hitting
the United States but they're certainly working

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:11.670
on it.

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:17.240
Do we have a point at which we will not just
stand for-

00:44:17.240 --> 00:44:18.760
CARTER: Well, we certainly have-
BOIES: -their activities?

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:29.220
CARTER: We certainly have a defensive plan,
but it extends beyond the Korean Peninsula

00:44:29.220 --> 00:44:30.740
with respect to North Korea.

00:44:30.740 --> 00:44:34.190
As I said, their behavior is unpredictable.

00:44:34.190 --> 00:44:40.130
I would be imprudent as a secretary of defense
to assume that North Korea isn't going to

00:44:40.130 --> 00:44:41.730
continue to make progress.

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:49.790
Now, we will, ourselves, and with the help
of the Chinese, try to make that less likely,

00:44:49.790 --> 00:44:56.740
but we're taking-and we actually made this
decision several years ago to increase the

00:44:56.740 --> 00:45:03.090
number and the capabilities of our defenses,
including our missile defenses, of the Continental

00:45:03.090 --> 00:45:06.480
United States, of Alaska, of Hawaii.

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:13.190
And so we're taking steps, so we are-it definitely
has consequences for us and we're doing what

00:45:13.190 --> 00:45:14.190
we have to do.

00:45:14.190 --> 00:45:18.900
It's part of our investments in our posture
for the future there.

00:45:18.900 --> 00:45:25.500
BOIES: You mentioned India, and you have certainly
personally put a lot of time and attention

00:45:25.500 --> 00:45:32.220
into building what for us would be a very
important and relatively new, strong alliance.

00:45:32.220 --> 00:45:40.170
You say that we may have what you characterize
as difficult negotiations over co-production

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:46.150
and co-development of military capability
such as fighter airplanes.

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:49.380
What is India going to want that we're not
going to give them?

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:50.910
CARTER: Well, it's just difficult in the sense
of it's business.

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:55.510
BOIES: And there are technology transfer issues
involved.

00:45:55.510 --> 00:45:56.540
CARTER: There are.

00:45:56.540 --> 00:45:57.540
There are.

00:45:57.540 --> 00:46:03.050
In these cases I think that there are some
of those issues, but really you've got to

00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:07.800
understand the challenges here I think are
surmountable, but they derive from history

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:11.260
more than they derive from today's logic.

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:19.040
The history goes like this: The United States
and India, for 50 years, since after Indian

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:23.950
independence, essentially lived apart.

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:29.020
India's policy was one of nonalignment in
the Cold War.

00:46:29.020 --> 00:46:35.780
They wouldn't do with us the kinds of things
that we're doing today-together today.

00:46:35.780 --> 00:46:41.330
To the extent they had a technology relationship-military
technology relationship with another country,

00:46:41.330 --> 00:46:44.180
it was mostly the Soviet Union.

00:46:44.180 --> 00:46:51.600
So their entire military industrial system
and ours grew up very differently and remain

00:46:51.600 --> 00:46:52.600
quite different.

00:46:52.600 --> 00:46:56.380
So you're talking about taking two things
that are really quite different and trying

00:46:56.380 --> 00:46:58.330
to match them up and make them work together.

00:46:58.330 --> 00:47:06.170
And a lot of the difficulties that-in just
making the U.S.-India defense relationship

00:47:06.170 --> 00:47:10.119
go forward are simply of that nature.

00:47:10.119 --> 00:47:19.510
But there's no difficulty in principle either
with the idea, in our minds, that India is

00:47:19.510 --> 00:47:28.150
an enduring partner, or in their minds their
willingness to work with us, although in a

00:47:28.150 --> 00:47:31.580
way that retains their independence, which
is important.

00:47:31.580 --> 00:47:32.580
BOIES: Right.

00:47:32.580 --> 00:47:35.310
CARTER: Of course we don't have any problem
with that.

00:47:35.310 --> 00:47:39.560
When it gets down to issues of co-production
and co-development, I mean, there you get

00:47:39.560 --> 00:47:45.650
into, you know, situations where it's not
just technology but these are American companies.

00:47:45.650 --> 00:47:49.720
And we don't do the negotiations for those
companies; they do them themselves.

00:47:49.720 --> 00:47:55.380
But, you know, my job is to make those kind
of negotiations less difficult by making sure

00:47:55.380 --> 00:48:04.960
that this 50 years of history is overcome
in the bureaucratic and administrative reflex

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:14.080
sense, and that the tremendously bright opportunity
of this huge place, a billion people, and

00:48:14.080 --> 00:48:17.820
the United States in decades to come-as I
said, I use the word "destiny."

00:48:17.820 --> 00:48:26.609
I see there's no question about where the
United States-India relationship is going.

00:48:26.609 --> 00:48:32.170
We can control and influence the pace, and
I want to do that.

00:48:32.170 --> 00:48:39.330
BOIES: My personal view is that you will go
down as the most effective multi-tasker in

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:41.380
the history of secretaries of defense.

00:48:41.380 --> 00:48:42.380
(Laughter.)

00:48:42.380 --> 00:48:43.380
CARTER: I used to say that.

00:48:43.380 --> 00:48:50.109
BOIES: And in that regard, you have started
consortiums with Silicon Valley; you've enlisted

00:48:50.109 --> 00:48:56.300
Eric Schmidt to chair a committee that will
do a lot of future looking.

00:48:56.300 --> 00:49:00.910
Just the other day you were at MIT announcing
a consortium to develop fabrics-

00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:01.910
CARTER: Yeah.

00:49:01.910 --> 00:49:03.360
BOIES: -that will be of military use.

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:07.090
Tell us about these innovative projects and
what we can look forward to seeing.

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:13.210
CARTER: Well, one of the strengths-you know,
what makes ours the finest fighting force

00:49:13.210 --> 00:49:15.130
the world has ever known?

00:49:15.130 --> 00:49:16.820
Well, first it's our people.

00:49:16.820 --> 00:49:23.180
And I have to tend to that so that 10 years,
20 years, 30 years from now-it's an all-volunteer

00:49:23.180 --> 00:49:24.180
force.

00:49:24.180 --> 00:49:25.450
We don't make anybody do this.

00:49:25.450 --> 00:49:28.640
We continue to attract and retain great people.

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:34.609
But the second thing that makes-has made the
American military great over decades and decades

00:49:34.609 --> 00:49:39.030
is technology.

00:49:39.030 --> 00:49:46.040
In the old days, in the old Sputnik days,
much of the technology of consequence for

00:49:46.040 --> 00:49:52.630
military purposes was born in the United States
and actually under the United States government.

00:49:52.630 --> 00:49:53.890
We still do a lot.

00:49:53.890 --> 00:49:59.890
We're spending $72 billion on research and
development this year.

00:49:59.890 --> 00:50:08.280
But we're still-that's more than twice what
the major tech companies combined spent on

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:11.530
R&amp;D in a year.

00:50:11.530 --> 00:50:17.990
So were still a big dog, but it's a much more
diversified, industrial and technological

00:50:17.990 --> 00:50:18.990
landscape.

00:50:18.990 --> 00:50:21.760
And of course, it's more international and
it's more commercial.

00:50:21.760 --> 00:50:25.480
So if we're going to stay great in that sense,
we need to reach out.

00:50:25.480 --> 00:50:26.660
And that's what I'm trying to do.

00:50:26.660 --> 00:50:34.290
I'm trying to build new bridges to what we
know is a new, innovative culture.

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:36.930
It's not the same as it was in the '50s, and
'60s, and '70s.

00:50:36.930 --> 00:50:42.400
And glorious as our history is, with satellite
communications and the Internet, and all the

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:49.430
things that were born from defense innovation,
it's going to be different in the future.

00:50:49.430 --> 00:50:51.990
And I need to connect with those people.

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:55.960
When I do so, and that's what I was up there
doing in Boston in this area of fabrics, which

00:50:55.960 --> 00:51:00.540
may sound like a niche, but it's not really
if you think about being able to wear clothing

00:51:00.540 --> 00:51:09.000
that will be your batteries, charge your batteries,
and make it difficult to detect and see you,

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:12.970
and regulate your temperature whether it's
cold or-all these things are now possible.

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:18.830
So it's just one-and, by the way, one that
is of importance to the economy more generally.

00:51:18.830 --> 00:51:23.660
So where I can create that unity in a new
way, I really need to do that.

00:51:23.660 --> 00:51:27.400
I owe that to my successor and my successor's
successor.

00:51:27.400 --> 00:51:33.270
I find, when I do that, that the tech community
of all kinds-it's not just Silicon Valley

00:51:33.270 --> 00:51:35.980
and it's not just tech-tech, you know, the
IT.

00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:38.360
It's sort of biotech.

00:51:38.360 --> 00:51:41.050
Boston is a big hub of that.

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:44.640
Manufacturing technology, which is the purpose
of the manufacturing institute that we funded

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:53.099
up in the Boston area, which is number eight
of eight that we've done of that kind.

00:51:53.099 --> 00:52:03.020
I find that the innovators of America are
very welcoming of that.

00:52:03.020 --> 00:52:06.420
And they-first of all, they recognize that
it's serious business.

00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:08.780
Security-it's not a game.

00:52:08.780 --> 00:52:10.720
And they recognize that.

00:52:10.720 --> 00:52:14.790
And secondly, these are people who like to
do things that matter.

00:52:14.790 --> 00:52:19.040
And protecting our people and leaving a better
world for our children, that really matters.

00:52:19.040 --> 00:52:20.730
So I find the uptake is really high.

00:52:20.730 --> 00:52:25.040
And it's just-and you know, every time I'm
doing something in family programs, women

00:52:25.040 --> 00:52:27.520
in the military, and so forth, they say, why
are you doing these things?

00:52:27.520 --> 00:52:34.010
I'm doing them because I want to make sure
that we remain the finest fighting force the

00:52:34.010 --> 00:52:35.050
world has ever known.

00:52:35.050 --> 00:52:42.900
To that, I need to keep up with how generations
change, how technology changes, and make sure

00:52:42.900 --> 00:52:49.190
that, as I constantly tell our people, we're
thinking outside of our five-sided box of

00:52:49.190 --> 00:52:50.500
the Pentagon.

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:51.500
(Laughter.)

00:52:51.500 --> 00:52:54.680
BOIES: I would like to open for questions.

00:52:54.680 --> 00:52:57.750
Please state your name and your affiliation.

00:52:57.750 --> 00:53:02.830
And please, try to keep your question shorter
and more to the point than mine.

00:53:02.830 --> 00:53:03.830
(Laughter.)

00:53:03.830 --> 00:53:05.240
Yes, sir.

00:53:05.240 --> 00:53:10.119
Q: George Schwab, National Committee on American
Foreign Policy.

00:53:10.119 --> 00:53:12.410
Thank you very much, Mr. Secretary.

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:19.310
I wonder, in your deterrence-in our deterrence
policy, what role does Taiwan play?

00:53:19.310 --> 00:53:26.270
CARTER: Well, Taiwan is a good trading partner
of the United States.

00:53:26.270 --> 00:53:32.740
Our policy there derives from-and this gets
down into the sort of lore of past times,

00:53:32.740 --> 00:53:37.470
but it still is the basis of our policy, has
been for decades and it's worked-goes back

00:53:37.470 --> 00:53:44.490
to the so-called three communiques between
the United States and China, which settled

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:50.100
between us the issue of was Taiwan-was there
one China, and so forth.

00:53:50.100 --> 00:53:57.130
And that policy has been established now-it
goes back to several administrations.

00:53:57.130 --> 00:54:03.850
It's been sustained through that time, and
by successive governments of Taiwan, and by

00:54:03.850 --> 00:54:07.230
successive governments in China as a good
thing.

00:54:07.230 --> 00:54:14.760
And the other is the Taiwan Relation Act,
which instructs us, and me as the secretary

00:54:14.760 --> 00:54:22.760
of defense, to maintain the capability to
assist Taiwan in its own defense.

00:54:22.760 --> 00:54:24.470
So we do that.

00:54:24.470 --> 00:54:29.750
But in the main, our policy derives from the
three communiques.

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:35.540
And that situation has been a peaceful one
now for a number of decades.

00:54:35.540 --> 00:54:39.430
And if you go back to the 1950s, you know,
you will remember, there was a real prospect

00:54:39.430 --> 00:54:41.849
of conflict over Taiwan.

00:54:41.849 --> 00:54:46.210
So it's an example, if you like, of something
that takes a long time.

00:54:46.210 --> 00:54:49.470
And, of course, it hasn't been landed yet.

00:54:49.470 --> 00:54:57.119
But we've kept the peace in a way that has
allowed Taiwan to certainly develop, in its

00:54:57.119 --> 00:55:04.810
own way, and kept it peaceful, in the peace
between the United States and China and Taiwan

00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:06.920
and others in the region.

00:55:06.920 --> 00:55:07.920
So that's the story.

00:55:07.920 --> 00:55:09.130
BOIES: I'm going to take two questions.

00:55:09.130 --> 00:55:10.930
Yes, sir, in the front.

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:18.260
And yes, sir, in the middle.

00:55:18.260 --> 00:55:19.260
Here.

00:55:19.260 --> 00:55:20.260
Q: I don't have a microphone, so I'll speak
loudly.

00:55:20.260 --> 00:55:21.260
BOIES: You need a microphone, sir.

00:55:21.260 --> 00:55:22.480
Q: David Rivkin, president of the International
Bar Association.

00:55:22.480 --> 00:55:25.630
I applaud your work with India.

00:55:25.630 --> 00:55:29.300
You didn't mention at all how it might impact
our relations with Pakistan, which are so

00:55:29.300 --> 00:55:30.540
important for many other reasons.

00:55:30.540 --> 00:55:31.960
So I was wondering if you could talk about
that-

00:55:31.960 --> 00:55:32.960
CARTER: Sure.

00:55:32.960 --> 00:55:33.960
Q: -for just a minute.

00:55:33.960 --> 00:55:34.960
CARTER: Sure.

00:55:34.960 --> 00:55:35.960
Sure.

00:55:35.960 --> 00:55:36.960
Well-
BOIES: Before you do, can I get another question

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:37.960
in?

00:55:37.960 --> 00:55:38.960
CARTER: Yeah.

00:55:38.960 --> 00:55:39.960
BOIES: You in the middle.

00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:40.960
Sir.

00:55:40.960 --> 00:55:42.530
Q: Secretary, Jason Sherman from Inside Defense.

00:55:42.530 --> 00:55:47.440
As the department thinks about this region,
I wonder if you could say a word or two about

00:55:47.440 --> 00:55:52.599
a relatively new entity stood up in the Office
of Secretary of Defense called the China Strategic

00:55:52.599 --> 00:55:57.750
Initiative, about its composition, its role
in policymaking, and planning and budgeting.

00:55:57.750 --> 00:55:58.750
Thank you.

00:55:58.750 --> 00:55:59.750
CARTER: OK.

00:55:59.750 --> 00:56:07.200
Well, first of all, with respect to Pakistan,
that also is an important security partner;

00:56:07.200 --> 00:56:17.160
a whole lot of issues, of which counterterrorism
looms largest.

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:22.060
And we work with the Pakistanis all the time
on that.

00:56:22.060 --> 00:56:32.570
It's long past-we're long past the point in
U.S. policymaking where we look at the India-Pakistan

00:56:32.570 --> 00:56:36.720
dyad as the whole story for either one of
them.

00:56:36.720 --> 00:56:41.570
We have much more to do with India today than
has to do with Pakistan.

00:56:41.570 --> 00:56:46.610
There's important business with respect to
Pakistan, but we have much more-a whole global

00:56:46.610 --> 00:56:53.710
agenda with India, an agenda that covers all
kinds of issues.

00:56:53.710 --> 00:56:58.480
With respect to Pakistan, once again, totally
different.

00:56:58.480 --> 00:57:03.830
We have a big set of issues having to do with
the border with Afghanistan, where we continue

00:57:03.830 --> 00:57:11.630
to operate; with terrorism, both on the territory
of Pakistan and also obviously cross-border

00:57:11.630 --> 00:57:17.510
into Afghanistan, including affecting U.S.
service members there.

00:57:17.510 --> 00:57:21.990
And so I'm sure I'll be asked about it in
India.

00:57:21.990 --> 00:57:27.970
But the first thing one needs to say, from
an American policy point of view, these are

00:57:27.970 --> 00:57:32.550
both respected partners and friends.

00:57:32.550 --> 00:57:36.640
They find themselves in very different situations.

00:57:36.640 --> 00:57:44.160
And the days are gone when we only deal with
India as the other side of the Pakistan coin,

00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:46.640
or Pakistan as the other side of the India
coin.

00:57:46.640 --> 00:57:52.440
I know that there are those in India and Pakistan
who are still glued to that dyad way of thinking.

00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:55.690
But the United States put that behind us some
time ago.

00:57:55.690 --> 00:57:58.520
BOIES: And then the question about China.

00:57:58.520 --> 00:58:04.810
CARTER: Well, I don't know specifically what
is being referred to there, but we have a

00:58:04.810 --> 00:58:09.830
number of ways I've described-they weren't
described today, but the things that we're

00:58:09.830 --> 00:58:19.890
doing to underwrite our ambition to have a
stronger relationship with China in the military-to-military

00:58:19.890 --> 00:58:20.890
area.

00:58:20.890 --> 00:58:24.540
Q: (Off mic)-but it's a red team that-
CARTER: I believe that's possible.

00:58:24.540 --> 00:58:27.480
Q: It's a red team at OSD.

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:29.580
It's the China Strategic Initiative?

00:58:29.580 --> 00:58:31.880
CARTER: Well, I'm afraid I don't know.

00:58:31.880 --> 00:58:36.060
There are lots of teams around, and particularly
ones looking at China.

00:58:36.060 --> 00:58:38.601
So I don't know what this particular one is.

00:58:38.601 --> 00:58:41.570
BOIES: My cheap watch-
CARTER: I hope it's doing good work.

00:58:41.570 --> 00:58:42.590
(Laughter.)

00:58:42.590 --> 00:58:45.940
BOIES: My cheap watch does not match the clock.

00:58:45.940 --> 00:58:48.370
How are we for time?

00:58:48.370 --> 00:58:51.410
We've got-do we have time for more questions?

00:58:51.410 --> 00:58:52.410
MR.

00:58:52.410 --> 00:58:53.410
: I'm really sorry.

00:58:53.410 --> 00:58:54.410
We got to-
BOIES: OK.

00:58:54.410 --> 00:58:56.310
We have a hard stop, I am very sorry to say.

00:58:56.310 --> 00:58:57.310
Mr. Secretary, thank you so much.

00:58:57.310 --> 00:58:58.310
CARTER: Thank you.

00:58:58.310 --> 00:58:59.310
It's good to be with you.

00:58:59.310 --> 00:59:00.310
Appreciate it.

00:59:00.310 --> 00:59:01.310
(Applause.)

00:59:01.310 --> 00:59:02.310
Good to be with you.

00:59:02.310 --> 00:59:03.310
(Applause.)

00:59:03.310 --> 00:59:03.311
(END)

